  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale:\n   This optimization strategy combines two phases: (1) Adaptive multi-length exploration with targeted initialization, and (2) Progressive smoothing and gradient-based refinement. Unlike the standard approach, we prioritize length diversity while incorporating sophisticated gradient analysis from the entire convolution spectrum. The key innovation is the dual-temporal operation: initial coarse searches followed by domain-specific gradient guidance. This leverages the observation that longer sequences have more structural freedom to diminish peaks, while shorter sequences are computationally feasible for gradient analysis. The method performs a controlled doubling of lengths, with financial safeguards against excessive FLOPs.\n\n2. Mathematical Specification:\n   We optimize the sequence a = (a0,...,an-1) subject to:\n   \u2016a\u2016_\u221e \u2264 1000 (constrained)\n   a_i \u2265 0 (nonnegativity)\n   Let q = n (length) and r = 2q-1 (convolution dimensionality)\n   The objective is f(a) = (2n * max(convolve_full(a,a))) / (sum(a))\u00b2, constructed via a three-stage process:\n   Stage1: Multi-phase initialization (cf. existing software) including exponential and asymmetric Gaussian forms.\n   Stage2: Smooth minimization with temperature control\n   Stage3: Frank-Wolfe refinement with greedy vertex selection based on 100% FFT-convolved gradient feedback\n\n3. Implementation Procedure:\n   - Initialization (200 seconds):\n     Generate an initial candidate from a length-optimized two-phase process\n     Candidates include: asymmetric Gaussian (time-adjusted), bell curve (width-adaptive), and uniform exponential decay\n\n   -Phase1: Variable-Length Adam Optimization (550 seconds):\n     Repeat 3-5 times:\n     -Exhaust over lengths: 512, 768, 1024, 1280, 1536\n     -Use gradient checking via full convolution to independently gauge improvements\n     -Track learning rate decay (automatic) and momentum stabilization\n     -Utilize peak-reduction strategies from LP-based exterior penalties\n\n   -Phase2: Semi-Smooth Scheduled Frank-Wolfe (600 seconds):\n     With learning rate = 0.08 per iteration\n     Terminate refinement at plateau detection\n     Integrate financial constraints (peak-reduction thresholds)\n\n   -Geometry Optimization (450 seconds):\n     Use gradient descent coupled with targeted geometric updates\n     Maintain peak monotonic reduction and smooth optimization\n     Apply gradient checks throughout the space\n\n   4. Search Choices and Variations:\n      Primary hyperparameter search ranges:\n      Initialization limit: 200 seconds (range heights)\n      Adam iterations: 300 per length (temperature: 0.05-0.15)\n      Frank-Wolfe restarts: 0.95 coefficient space adjustment\n      Length doubling: binary search space with exponential bounds\n      Gaussian center bias: parameterized offsets (version-controlled)\n\n   5. Compute Budget and Robustness:\n      The algorithm executes 4-6 timed polishing steps with validation\n      Each two-phase run scales O(n log n) designed\n      Critical timing checkpoints at 20q, 15q, 8q, 5q, 3q steps\n\n   6. Validation and Return Contract:\n      Maintain an optimal energy state throughout Frank-Wolfe iterations\n      Final verification must stop at 0.03% error margin\n      Sequence inductively proved finite through FFT computation\n      Reconstruction potential exists via gradient projection verification\n      Sequence representation must support both initial and refined positions\n      All operations must maintain positivity and smoothness constraints\n\nA. Primary initialization:\n   Input sequencing may be binary-coded with technical indicator criteria\n   Genetic algorithm selection preserves physics-based maximum reduction\n\nB. Progressive optimization:\n   -Phase1: Adam smoothing (\u9ad8\u901f\u589e\u957f\u6bb5)\n   -Phase2: Frank-Wolfe polishing (subspace reduction)\n   -Phase3: Financial/lifetime optimization (error-margin breathing room)\n\nH.L MK, 2023-10-27\nOptimization version 2.5 \u2014 Python3 $motion-type-frame",
